{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ה' ימלוך", :translit "Hashem yimlokh", :gloss "YHWH will reign"} {:he "ה' מלך ה' מלך ה' ימלוך לעולם ועד", :translit "Hashem malakh, Hashem malakh, Hashem yimlokh le'olam va'ed", :gloss "YHWH reigned, YHWH reigns, YHWH will reign forever and ever"} {:he "מלכות או\"ה", :translit "malkhus Edom-Romi", :gloss "kingdom of Esau-Rome"} {:he "ימלוך ה' לעולם", :translit "yimlokh Hashem le'olam", :gloss "YHWH will reign forever"} {:he "ודרך רשעים יעות", :translit "vederekh resha'im yish'ol", :gloss "and the way of the wicked shall be crooked"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Pesik (vertical stroke) after 'YHWH will reign' signals a liturgical-midrashic break tied to the threefold formula of divine kingship."}], :frame {:text "The threefold acclamation 'YHWH reigned, YHWH reigns, YHWH will reign forever and ever' is recited on Rosh Ha-Shanah and appears in the liturgy as part of Malkhuyot verses (Rosh Ha-Shanah 32a). The motif that God's eternal kingship will displace the four exiles, climaxing with the fall of Esau-Rome, is a standard eschatological theme found in midrashic sources such as Tanhuma. The observation that Moses writes 'YHWH will reign' (placing the divine name before kingship) while David writes 'YHWH will reign forever' in Psalms 146:10 (with the verb first, placing kingship before the name) reflects a literary comparison of syntactic ordering across these two verses.", :sources ["Rosh Ha-Shanah 32a" "Psalms 146:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Pesik (vertical stroke) after 'YHWH will reign' signals a liturgical-midrashic break tied to the threefold formula of divine kingship.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:16:22.016766642Z", :model "glm-5.2"}